turns.text:
## The problem

The error is a type mismatch that comes straight out of Rust's ownership model:

```
error[E0308]: mismatched types
 --> src/lib.rs:9:5
  |
9 |     best
  |     ^^^^ expected `String`, found `&str`
```

`best` is a `&str` — a *borrowed view* into memory owned by whatever `text` points at. The signature promises to hand back a `String`, which is an **owned**, heap-allocated buffer the caller becomes responsible for freeing.

You can't turn a borrow into ownership for free. `longest_word` never owns the character data (it only got a reference in), so it has nothing to give away. The only options are:

1. Change the return type to `&str` and tie it to the input's lifetime (`fn longest_word<'a>(text: &'a str) -> &'a str`) — the caller keeps owning the data, or
2. **Copy** the selected slice into a new heap allocation with `.to_string()` / `.to_owned()`.

Since the signature is fixed, we do #2. Note this is exactly why Rust makes you write the conversion explicitly: it's an allocation and a memcpy, not a free cast.

Two smaller notes:
- `let words: Vec<&str> = ... .collect()` allocates a whole vector just to iterate it once. You can iterate the `SplitWhitespace` iterator directly.
- Empty input needs no special case: `split_whitespace` yields nothing, `best` stays `""`, and `"".to_string()` is an empty `String` (which doesn't even allocate).

## Fixed version

```rust
fn longest_word(text: &str) -> String {
    let mut best: &str = "";
    for w in text.split_whitespace() {
        if w.len() > best.len() {
            best = w;
        }
    }
    // `best` borrows from `text`; copy it into an owned String to return it.
    best.to_string()
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::longest_word;

    #[test]
    fn empty_input() {
        assert_eq!(longest_word(""), "");
        assert_eq!(longest_word("   \t\n "), "");
    }

    #[test]
    fn picks_longest() {
        assert_eq!(longest_word("a bb ccc dd"), "ccc");
    }

    #[test]
    fn ties_keep_first() {
        assert_eq!(longest_word("aaa bbb"), "aaa");
    }

    #[test]
    fn handles_extra_whitespace() {
        assert_eq!(longest_word("  hello   worldly  "), "worldly");
    }
}
```

## Idiomatic alternative

```rust
fn longest_word(text: &str) -> String {
    text.split_whitespace()
        .max_by_key(|w| w.len())
        .unwrap_or("")
        .to_string()
}
```

Careful: `max_by_key` returns the **last** maximum on ties, whereas the original loop (using strict `>`) keeps the **first**. If tie-breaking matters, keep the explicit loop or use `.fold("", |best, w| if w.len() > best.len() { w } else { best })`.

Also, `w.len()` is a count of **bytes**, not characters. For "longest" by user-perceived length with non-ASCII text, use `w.chars().count()`.

